So we're gonna go ahead and
show you how to publish this

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:03.150
application to Posit Connect.

00:26:04.250 --> 00:26:06.515
Now here within
the RStudio IDE,

00:26:06.515 --> 00:26:09.795
you can actually implement something
known as push button deployment.

00:26:09.795 --> 00:26:11.975
And we showed this in some
of our previous workflows.

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:15.590
Here's that little blue icon
on the top of your screen,

00:26:15.590 --> 00:26:18.410
which you can easily
push, and I could manually

00:26:19.190 --> 00:26:22.495
publish this to Posit Connect
And this is a great option,

00:26:22.495 --> 00:26:26.255
especially if you just want
to quickly share an application

00:26:26.255 --> 00:26:29.315
with somebody or any piece of
content that can be hosted on Connect.

00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:30.860
However,

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:33.900
if I publish this to connect
and then I come back to my

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:35.980
source code and
I make a change,

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:38.960
I'm going to have to
click the button again and republish.

00:26:39.525 --> 00:26:41.385
So it's a bit of
a manual process.

00:26:42.165 --> 00:26:44.645
So what we're gonna show
you here is something called

00:26:44.645 --> 00:26:46.005
git-backed deployment.

00:26:46.005 --> 00:26:49.430
So instead of publishing it
directly from the rstudio IDE,

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:52.790
we're actually just gonna
have Posit Connect look at our

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:53.690
GitHub repository.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:57.910
And if it ever detects a
change in a GitHub repository,

00:26:57.910 --> 00:26:59.705
so commit in that
GitHub repository,

00:27:00.245 --> 00:27:03.385
it can automatically
rebuild that content for us.

00:27:04.405 --> 00:27:07.140
So to kick off the get
back deployment process,

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:09.560
we have a few things we
need to do to get started.

00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:13.800
So I'm gonna go ahead and just clear
my console down here in the bottom left.

00:27:14.345 --> 00:27:17.465
And I want to go
into the directory that houses the

00:27:17.465 --> 00:27:20.985
content that we ultimately want
to be published on Connect.

00:27:20.985 --> 00:27:23.340
So here within that
test app directory,

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:26.700
You can see we just have
the single app.R file.

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:30.460
And I'm actually gonna set this
as my working directory just to

00:27:30.460 --> 00:27:31.885
make things a
little bit simpler.

00:27:33.165 --> 00:27:37.325
Now when we publish this
application to Posit Connect,

00:27:37.325 --> 00:27:41.240
Posit Connect needs to
know some information about my

00:27:41.240 --> 00:27:44.600
current working environment, such
as what packages am I

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:47.000
using? What versions
of those packages?

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:48.835
What are version am I using?

00:27:48.835 --> 00:27:52.915
So that information is needed by
connect so that it can, you know,

00:27:52.915 --> 00:27:56.615
just make sure it runs
this application correctly without any issues.

00:27:57.270 --> 00:27:59.590
Now for push button deployment,
this little blue button,

00:27:59.590 --> 00:28:01.270
a lot of that's
done automatically,

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:03.030
but if we're gonna use
git-back deployment,

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:07.335
we just need to provide that information
and it's super easy to do it.

00:28:07.335 --> 00:28:10.535
So from within the directory of
the content you want to publish,

00:28:10.535 --> 00:28:14.630
all you need to do is
run from the rsconnect package there's a

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:16.250
function called writeManifest.

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:18.810
That's it. I'll hit enter.

00:28:19.430 --> 00:28:21.990
It's gonna capture some of
my dependencies using another

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:25.495
really great package called
renv and then it generates this

00:28:25.495 --> 00:28:27.435
manifest dot JSON file.

00:28:28.215 --> 00:28:30.855
I can click into it, and we
can just take a quick peek,

00:28:30.855 --> 00:28:32.270
and it's pretty noisy.

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:34.510
There's a lot of
great information in here that

00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:37.310
connect can easily read,
but for the most part,

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:40.430
it's just information about packages
in my environment and, like,

00:28:40.430 --> 00:28:43.705
what versions of those packages
and where I obtained them from.

00:28:43.705 --> 00:28:46.185
All the information connect
needs to replicate my

00:28:46.185 --> 00:28:49.180
environment exactly as it is
right here within Posit workbench.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:51.980
So now that we've
created this file,

00:28:51.980 --> 00:28:55.980
we wanna make sure that we
also include this file within our

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:56.880
GitHub repository.

00:28:57.425 --> 00:28:59.745
So let's go ahead and just
show you that workflow one more

00:28:59.745 --> 00:29:02.625
time. So we have this manifest
dot JSON file that's been

00:29:02.625 --> 00:29:06.180
added, within my git repository
or within version control.

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:09.560
Let's go ahead and stage
it. I'm going to commit it,

00:29:10.580 --> 00:29:16.285
and we'll just make a quick
message here. Of add manifest file,

00:29:16.905 --> 00:29:18.925
and then we'll commit
that up to GitHub.

00:29:21.770 --> 00:29:24.190
So if I come back to
my GitHub repository,

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:26.110
we click on test app. We

00:29:27.770 --> 00:29:28.990
need a refresh here.

00:29:29.505 --> 00:29:32.065
Since now I've done something
wrong here. Oh, yeah.

00:29:32.065 --> 00:29:33.505
I gotta gotta push
it, obviously.

00:29:33.505 --> 00:29:35.045
So let's go ahead
and push that.

00:29:35.665 --> 00:29:38.600
There we go. So I committed
it, and then I had to push it.

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:41.240
Now if I refresh my
page, we have the app,

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:44.220
and we have the manifest
file right alongside of it.

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:48.415
So let's go ahead and take
this app application on my GitHub

00:29:48.415 --> 00:29:52.195
repository and publish it
directly from this GitHub repository.

00:29:52.735 --> 00:29:55.820
So I'm gonna regrab the
URL for the third time.

00:29:55.820 --> 00:29:59.920
And I'm gonna switch over to
Posit Connect here in this demo environment.

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:03.295
So this is the home
screen of Posit Connect.

00:30:03.295 --> 00:30:06.735
Just some example content that
we have here just to explore

00:30:06.735 --> 00:30:09.935
all the various pieces of
content you can currently host

00:30:09.935 --> 00:30:10.995
on Posit Connect.

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:14.440
But in the very top here,
you'll see this publish drop

00:30:14.440 --> 00:30:17.180
down, and I'm gonna
select import from git.

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:19.625
I'm gonna paste in the URL,

00:30:20.485 --> 00:30:22.425
and also hit click next.

00:30:23.205 --> 00:30:26.140
And it's gonna ask me what
branch I want to publish from.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:29.260
Now our GitHub repository
just has a single branch.

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:30.700
It's that main branch,

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:33.180
but there's actually a pretty
cool workflow here where you

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:37.305
can have multiple versions
of a single piece of content

00:30:37.305 --> 00:30:39.465
published on
different branches.

00:30:39.465 --> 00:30:42.745
So maybe you have like a
production version on the main branch,

00:30:42.745 --> 00:30:44.985
but maybe you have a
development version on some

00:30:44.985 --> 00:30:48.440
other you can actually publish
two pieces of content from the

00:30:48.440 --> 00:30:51.480
same GitHub repository
from different branches.

00:30:51.480 --> 00:30:54.325
But we just have one branch
that's main, so we'll select next.

00:30:55.605 --> 00:30:57.945
And then it's gonna
look for directories

00:30:58.565 --> 00:31:01.285
where you can find
that manifest dot JSON file.

00:31:01.285 --> 00:31:03.260
So these are
deployable directories.

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:06.380
And it found one inside
that test app directory,

00:31:06.380 --> 00:31:09.020
and all we have to do
is give our application a name. I'll

00:31:09.020 --> 00:31:12.035
say, test, app. We'll just
keep it nice and simple.

00:31:12.035 --> 00:31:13.655
And then we'll hit
deploy content.

00:31:20.610 --> 00:31:21.650
So at this point,

00:31:21.650 --> 00:31:24.850
Posit Connect is reading in
that manifest file and just

00:31:24.850 --> 00:31:27.095
making sure it can
replicate my environment.

00:31:27.095 --> 00:31:29.755
And once it's done, we
can click open content,

00:31:30.455 --> 00:31:32.875
and we'll just give it a
few seconds to boot up.

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:35.340
Alright.

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:38.460
So here is that shiny
application that we've been working on,

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:41.180
and you can actually see
the title here reflects that change

00:31:41.180 --> 00:31:44.585
that publisher two made
palmer, and it looks,

00:31:44.585 --> 00:31:48.765
and it behaves just like it did
when it was running within Posit Workbench.

00:31:51.620 --> 00:31:55.960
Now to demonstrate how powerful
Git-backed deployment is,

00:31:56.580 --> 00:31:59.320
so this application is now
linked to that GitHub repository.

00:32:00.165 --> 00:32:01.125
And if anyone,

00:32:01.125 --> 00:32:04.165
anyone I'm collaborating with
makes a change to the main

00:32:04.165 --> 00:32:05.705
branch on the
GitHub repository,

00:32:06.325 --> 00:32:09.520
it'll automatically be
reflected here within deployed

00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:13.440
content on Posit Connect.
So it'll automatically stay updated.

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:16.100
So let me actually go ahead
and show you that workflow quick.

00:32:17.435 --> 00:32:20.475
I'm gonna come back here to
my environment publisher one,

00:32:20.475 --> 00:32:21.595
but anyone can do this.

00:32:21.595 --> 00:32:24.475
Publisher two or anyone else
that's working on this GitHub

00:32:24.475 --> 00:32:27.420
repository and has access
to that main branch.

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:30.380
Let's go ahead and make
another change to the title.

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:32.160
So I have Palmer
penguins dashboard,

00:32:33.020 --> 00:32:34.880
and I'm just gonna add a dash

00:32:35.545 --> 00:32:38.665
Oh, let's just I'm gonna add
a smiley face. There we go.

00:32:38.665 --> 00:32:42.045
Kinda silly, but another
change. We'll hit save.

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:46.080
Now you can see in the git
tab, we have a modified this file.

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:49.440
So let's go ahead and stage
it. I'm going to commit it locally,

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:53.445
and I'll say add
smiley face. To title.

00:32:54.385 --> 00:32:56.085
And then we'll
commit this locally,

00:32:56.705 --> 00:32:58.485
and then push it up to GitHub.

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:03.860
So now if I switch back over
to my GitHub repository,

00:33:03.860 --> 00:33:05.940
let's just check the
application and make sure that

00:33:05.940 --> 00:33:06.840
change is reflected.

00:33:08.045 --> 00:33:08.365
Alright.

00:33:08.365 --> 00:33:11.565
So you can see the smiley
face right here on line twenty nine.

00:33:11.565 --> 00:33:14.125
Now I'm gonna switch back
over to Posit Connect.

00:33:14.125 --> 00:33:16.280
I'm gonna refresh my page here.

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:19.720
And you'll notice that the
smiley face isn't yet there.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:22.920
That's because this piece
of content, usually by default,

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:24.680
is only gonna
check every, like,

00:33:24.680 --> 00:33:27.975
fifteen minutes to see if there's
a change to that github repository.

00:33:27.975 --> 00:33:30.375
So we could just hang out
here for fifteen minutes and wait

00:33:30.375 --> 00:33:32.775
for that change to, take effect.

00:33:32.775 --> 00:33:37.500
And this is a time frame that
can be modified by your system administrators,

00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:40.220
but we can actually just
manually kick off that check.

00:33:40.220 --> 00:33:42.320
So if I go to my info tab here,

00:33:42.965 --> 00:33:46.165
scroll down, check
for updates from,

00:33:46.165 --> 00:33:48.325
periodically for tht
github repository,

00:33:48.325 --> 00:33:51.370
or I can just have it
manually check and click update now.

00:33:51.370 --> 00:33:52.990
Let's go ahead and
click this button.

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:57.770
So it sees says changes
were found in the git repository,

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:00.430
so it automatically
rebuilds it. Hit okay.

00:34:04.055 --> 00:34:07.815
And now you can see the smiley
face reflected here in our

00:34:07.815 --> 00:34:10.700
final, shiny
application. So again,

00:34:10.700 --> 00:34:14.140
git-backed deployment
is a fantastic way for,

00:34:14.140 --> 00:34:17.955
collaborating with others and also
having a piece of content

00:34:17.955 --> 00:34:20.915
hosted on Connect that's
automatically updated anytime

00:34:20.915 --> 00:34:24.375
someone makes a change to
that branch on the GitHub repository.

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:32.710
And that wraps up our last
Posit Team workflow of twenty twenty three.

00:34:32.710 --> 00:34:35.975
And I really hope you all found
today's session helpful for

00:34:35.975 --> 00:34:37.515
collaborative data science.

00:34:38.295 --> 00:34:39.895
Now usually for these sessions,

00:34:39.895 --> 00:34:43.180
we'd have a live question
and answer right afterwards.

00:34:43.180 --> 00:34:46.220
But just given the time of the
year and the fact that a lot of

00:34:46.220 --> 00:34:47.820
people are out of the office,

00:34:47.820 --> 00:34:50.540
we instead wanted to provide
you with another way to ask

00:34:50.540 --> 00:34:54.625
questions. So on the screen here
will be a link to a forum

00:34:54.625 --> 00:34:56.305
on Posit's Community page,

00:34:56.305 --> 00:34:59.285
which we'd welcome any
questions about today's content.

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:01.870
Finally, as a reminder,

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:05.230
all of these Posit Team demos
